    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for explaining that. so if the LDL is \"bad\" and it builds up in my arteries, how exactly does it do that? like is it just sitting there or is it actively doing something to them?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "LDL cholesterol actively contributes to the buildup of plaque inside your arteries. When LDL enters the bloodstream, it can become oxidized and stick to the walls of your arteries, forming a substance called plaque. Over time, this plaque can grow and harden, narrowing the arteries and restricting blood flow. This process is known as atherosclerosis. The reduced blood flow can cause your heart to work harder, potentially leading up to heart attacks, strokes, and other cardiovascular issues. Managing your LDL levels is important to prevent this problematic buildup and maintain healthy arteries and overall heart function.",
      "source": "target",
